              "inline_data": null,
              "text": "According to the **FDA Food Code** and **ServSafe standards**, the temperature at which food can be accepted during a delivery depends on the type of food. \n\n### 1. Cold TCS (Time/Temperature Control for Safety) Food: **41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower**\nMost cold foods (such as meat, poultry, fish, pre-cut produce, and dairy) must be received at **41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower**. \n\n### 2. Exceptions for Certain Cold Foods (**45\u00b0F / 7\u00b0C**)\nSome foods are allowed to be received at slightly higher temperatures, provided they meet specific storage conditions:\n* **Milk:** Can be accepted at **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C) or lower**, but must be cooled to 41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower within **4 hours** of delivery.\n* **Shell Eggs:** Can be received at an *ambient air temperature* of **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C) or lower**.\n* **Shucked Shellfish:** Can be accepted at **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C) or lower**, but must be cooled to 41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower within **4 hours**.\n* **Live Shellfish (oysters, clams, mussels, scallops):** Can be accepted at an *air temperature* of **45\u00b0F (7\u00b0C)** and an *internal temperature* of no more than **50\u00b0F (10\u00b0C)**. Once received, they must be cooled to 41\u00b0F (5\u00b0C) or lower within **4 hours**.\n\n### 3. Hot TCS Food: **135\u00b0F (57\u00b0C) or higher**\nAny hot, prepared foods (such as catering orders, pre-cooked buffet items, etc.) must be accepted at **135\u00b0F (57\u00b0C) or higher**. \n\n### 4. Frozen Food: **Frozen Solid**\nFrozen food does not have a specific numerical temperature limit, but it **must be received frozen solid**. \nYou should **reject** frozen food if you see:\n* Ice crystals or frozen liquids on the food or packaging (a sign of thawing and refreezing, which is temperature abuse).\n* Fluid stains or watermarks at the bottom of the cases or packaging.\n\n---\n\n### Best Practices for Receiving Food:\n* **Check the temperature immediately:** Use a calibrated thermometer to check the temperature of the food as soon as it arrives, before the delivery driver leaves.\n* **How to measure packaged food:** For vacuum-sealed or Reduced Oxygen Packaging (ROP), insert the thermometer probe *between* two packages rather than puncturing the seal. For other packaged foods, open the package and insert the probe directly into the food. \n* **Inspect the packaging:** Reject any food with damaged, torn, wet, or pest-infested packaging, even if the temperature is correct.",
